Problem Statement: Swap Two Numbers Description:
Given two integers, a and b, write a program to swap their values.
You must provide two approaches:
Standard Approach: Swap the numbers using a third temporary variable.

Bit Manipulation Approach: Swap the numbers in-place without using any extra variables by utilizing the bitwise XOR (^) operator.

Approach 1: Using a Temporary Variable
We temporarily store the value if a so it is not lost when we overwrite it with b.

void swapWithTemp(int &a, int &b) {
    int temp = a; //Store the original value of a 
    a = b; //Assign b to a 
    b = temp; //Assign the original value of a to b 
}

int main() {
    int a = 5; 
    int b = 10;

    cout << "Before swap (Temp): a = " << a << ", b = " << b << "\n";
    swapWithTemp(a,b);
    cout << "After swap (Temp): a = " << a << ", b = " << b << "\n";

    return 0;
}

TC: O(1)
coz the operations take a constant amount of time.

SC: O(1) 
auxiliary space though it expicity requires one extra variable (temp) in memory.

Approach 2: Uing Bitwise XOR 

void swapWithXOR(int &a, int &b) {
    //Check if the memory addresses are the same to prevent zeroing out the value 
    if(&a != &b) {
        a = a ^ b; //a now holds the XOR of original a and original b
        b = a ^ b; //b becomes (a ^ b) ^ b = a
        a = a ^ b; //a becomes (a ^ b) ^ a = b
    }
}

int main() {
    int a = 15;
    int b = 25;
    
    cout << "Before swap (XOR): a = " << a << ", b = " << b << "\n";
    swapWithXOR(a, b);
    cout << "After swap (XOR): a = " << a << ", b = " << b << "\n";
    
    return 0;
}

TC: O(1)
as it strictly executes 3 constant-time bitwise operator 

SC: O(1)
with absolutely zero extra variables 

